            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to go round, then the Latin for a detour."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: cōnspicor, cōnspicārī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cōnspicor, cōnspicārī — \"to catch sight of, to see\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Tandem viātōrēs mūrōs urbis cōnspicantur. At last the travellers catch sight of the city walls."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Deponent: cōnspicor, cōnspicārī, cōnspicātus sum, with an accusative. A related active verb is cōnspiciō."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cōnspicor is built from con- and the root of speciō 'to look' (Lewis & Short), the root seen in spectō 'to watch'. EtymDB lists it but gives no parent."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
