          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "revertor, revertī — \"to turn back, to return\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Via clausa est; itaque viātōrēs domum revertuntur. The road is closed; so the travellers turn back home."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Deponent in the present: revertor, revertī, but the perfect is active: revertī, reversus as a participle. English revert comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "revertor is built from re- 'back' and vertō 'to turn' (EtymDB; Lewis & Short, under revertō). English revert comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
